INT. HAUNTED HOUSE HOTLINE CALL CENTER - OPEN OFFICE - DAY

Cubicles under wire-hung plastic bats. A cardboard skeleton waves from the reception desk. Motivational posters read "ENGAGE YOUR SPIRIT." A whiteboard counts dead calls.

VERNON (50s, regional manager, a lanyard bigger than his head) wanders the aisle with a clipboard. PRIYA (30s, headset, fluorescent highlighter) types fast. CHAD (20s, sweatpants, energy-drink can) slumps. MS. LINDQVIST (60s, perfectly pressed blouse, glasses on a chain) sits at the corner desk, immaculate.

VERNON
Team! Quick huddle.

PRIYA
We're on calls, Vernon.

VERNON
It'll just take a minute.

CHAD
That's what you said about the pizza party.

VERNON
Which was a success.

MS. LINDQVIST
It was a Tuesday, Vernon. Nobody came.

VERNON
Because they were busy, Ms. Lindqvist.

MS. LINDQVIST
With what?

VERNON
Hauntings.

PRIYA
We got four calls yesterday. Three were wrong numbers.

VERNON
And the fourth?

PRIYA
A man wanted to know if we do exorcisms.

VERNON
Do we?

CHAD
We do a free consultation.

VERNON
Excellent. That's growth.

MS. LINDQVIST
It's one consultation.

VERNON
It's the beginning of a pipeline.

CHAD
Is the pipeline cursed?

VERNON
Chad.

CHAD
I'm just asking.

PRIYA
Vernon, with respect, why does the quarterly deck have a slide titled "Ghost Equity"?

VERNON
It's a value proposition.

MS. LINDQVIST
It's a pun.

VERNON
It's both.

The phones ring, all four at once. They stare. Nobody picks up. Vernon clears his throat. Straightens his tie. Smiles with all his teeth.

VERNON (CONT'D)
Okay! Let's get those calls.

PRIYA
They're ringing, Vernon.

VERNON
I can hear that.

CHAD
Who's answering?

MS. LINDQVIST
Not me.

They keep ringing. The lights flicker. The cardboard skeleton at reception topples over.

PRIYA
(very quietly)
Okay. That one's us.

VERNON
Let's hold on.

CHAD
(whispering)
Hold on to what?

VERNON
The scripts.

He picks up a laminated page. Reads. His voice cracks.

VERNON (CONT'D)
"Thank you for calling Spirit Assist. Your call is important to us."

MS. LINDQVIST
Don't say that to them.

VERNON
It's the script!

PRIYA
They're not callers, Vernon.

The phones stop. Silence. Then, from every headset on every desk, a whisper, in unison.

CHAD
Nope.

He stands. Takes his coat. Walks out. Priya looks at Ms. Lindqvist. Ms. Lindqvist takes off her glasses, folds them, and sets them on the desk.

MS. LINDQVIST
I'll start with the ones in the supply closet.
